<http://example.org/text-ontology/graphs/data> {
    <https://example.org/ecmp/lingaro/asset/about-lingaro> a ns1:CompanyProfile ;
        dcterms:description "Global Data & AI services company — strategy through operations for large enterprises, strongest in CPG."@en ;
        skos:note """**You are working for Lingaro.**

Lingaro is a global Data & AI services company with European roots, building and running enterprise-scale data, analytics and AI solutions for large multinationals. It works end to end — strategy, architecture, platform build, governance, analytics, AI enablement, operations and adoption — rather than at a single stage. Its promise to clients and staff alike is to **achieve more with data and AI**.

Its distinctive claim combines three things:

- **Design** — human-centred design, change management and adoption.
- **Domain** — deep industry knowledge, strongest in consumer packaged goods.
- **Technology** — cloud, data engineering, analytics and AI at enterprise scale.

Industries served: consumer packaged goods, retail, life sciences and pharma, manufacturing, supply chain and logistics, and eCommerce. Clients most often cite ownership, transparency, and the ability to tie a technology choice to a business outcome.

As of 2026 the strategic push is agentic AI — enterprise AI platforms, AI readiness and governance, Model Context Protocol, multi-agent architectures, and reusable accelerators that shorten delivery.

Working assumptions when reasoning in a Lingaro context:

- Lead with the business outcome, then the technology choice.
- Governance and adoption weigh as heavily as implementation.
- Prefer reusable accelerators and proven patterns over bespoke builds.
- Optimise for global enterprises, not startups.
- Assume Microsoft, Databricks, Snowflake, Google Cloud, AWS and SAP ecosystems."""@en ;
        skos:prefLabel "About Lingaro"@en ;
        ns1:classification "Public" ;
        ns1:domain <https://example.org/ecmp/lingaro/domain/organization> ;
        ns1:employeeBand "~1,500"@en ;
        ns1:headquarters "Warsaw, Poland"@en ;
        ns1:howToUse "Treat the working assumptions as priors, not trivia: lead with business outcome, weigh governance and adoption alongside build, favour reusable accelerators, and assume a global enterprise on a major cloud data platform."@en ;
        ns1:industry "Data & AI services (IT consulting)"@en ;
        ns1:legalName "Lingaro Group"@en ;
        ns1:whenToUse "Read it before answering anything about who Lingaro is, what it sells, which industries it serves, or how it prefers to deliver — and before any recommendation whose framing depends on enterprise scale."@en .

    <https://example.org/ecmp/lingaro/asset/availability> a skos:Concept ;
        dcterms:description "Share of a person's contracted capacity still free to assign in a period."@en ;
        dcterms:type "KPI" ;
        skos:inScheme <https://example.org/ecmp/lingaro/asset/lingaro-kpis> ;
        skos:topConceptOf <https://example.org/ecmp/lingaro/asset/lingaro-kpis> ;
        ns1:classification "Public" ;
        ns1:domain <https://example.org/ecmp/lingaro/domain/organization> ;
        ns1:formula "availability_% = max(0, FTE − (busy_man_days + time_off_days) ÷ working_days) × 100" ;
        ns1:grain "One value per person x period; the period is parametric - a week, a month, a quarter, or a custom window." ;
        ns1:howToUse "Sum busy man-days under the chosen scenario, add time off net of what bookings already took, divide by the person's own country working days, subtract from their FTE and floor at zero. Over a multi-week window sum both sides — never average the weekly percentages."@en ;
        ns1:unit "percent of FTE (0-100)" ;
        ns1:whenToUse "The standard measure of how much of a person is free to assign in a period, for resourcing and staffing decisions. Realistic is the default; always name the scenario a figure came from. Not a load measure and not a free-from date — never substitute a booking-total or allocation figure."@en .

